Dogs forward reveals battle with kidney disease Western Bulldogs forward Richelle Cranston has revealed she’s been living, and playing, with stage five chronic kidney disease. “I still manage to do the things I love,” the inaugural AFLW player posted to Instagram. “It’s just really hard sometimes.” Richelle Cranston of the Bulldogs: “It’s just really hard sometimes.” Credit: Getty Images The 34-year-old posted the caption along with a video detailing her life with the disease including weekly blood tests and daily blood pressure checks, tracking water and food intake, taking medications including resonium (to lower potassium levels), all while training in the high-performance environment.

The Dogs are Cranston’s third club after starting at Melbourne and having a stint with Geelong before heading to the Bulldogs last year. Cranston lined up for the Bulldogs in her side’s 57-point loss to Sydney on Saturday at Whitten Oval, 14.12 (96) to 6.3 (39).","entities":["Getty Images","The Age","Geelong","Comeback kings Richmond take the AFLW points in Cairns","Richelle Cranston","Western Bulldogs","stage five chronic kidney disease","resonium","Bulldogs' 57-point loss to Sydney","Melbourne","Cairns","Richmond","Sydney","Whitten Oval","AFLW"],"facts":["Which M.L.B. Playoff Team Has the Most Daunting Home Field? We Ranked Them. -MENTIONS-> Getty Images","Video games in 2023: Acquisitions, layoffs, unions -MENTIONS-> Getty Images","From Justin Timberlake to Sam Asghari: A timeline of Britney Spears’ relationships -MENTIONS-> Getty Images","West Indies v England: fifth and deciding men’s T20 cricket international – live -MENTIONS-> Getty Images","Amazon sellers sound off on the FTC's 'long-overdue' antitrust case -MENTIONS-> Getty Images"]}

