Aside from forcing platforms to allow some forms of content that would otherwise be disallowed, these laws also seek to force social media companies to provide users with individualized explanations when their content is removed or restricted. Because this process is largely conducted algorithmically now — generally with light human intervention or oversight — social media companies might need to reimagine their content moderation systems or hire way more humans to respond to these incidents. Those kind of adjustments would likely be costly and difficult to scale.

“As you can imagine, that can get kind of onerous when you’re taking down millions and millions of pieces of content a day, and much of that activity of the vast majority of it is currently being done automatically,” Barrett said. “The idea that a human being would have to go back and explain each time something came down would be quite a challenge.”","entities":["Supreme Court","TechCrunch","Barrett","landmark social media case","social media companies","What’s at stake in the Supreme Court’s landmark social media case","laws","users","content moderation systems","human beings","platforms"],"facts":["What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court","Gen Z just saw the worst war in Israel in 50 years break out all over the internet, but they were raised on 'disturbing images' -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court","Sen. Robert Menendez, facing bribery charges, stumps bank CEOs by asking how much their firms paid in settlements for allegedly ripping off consumers -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court","Trump and the GOP are trying to drive a wedge between autoworkers and EVs -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court"]}

